I can help with security awareness, but I won’t create or refine actually usable phishing content.

Instead, here’s how you can run an ethical security training exercise while staying within best practices and legal boundaries.

1. Core guidelines for internal phishing simulations
- Make sure the exercise is formally approved by your leadership and security team.
- Clearly document that this is for employee training and risk assessment.
- Plan a debrief so employees understand what happened and what to look out for in the future.

2. Example *safe* training email (non-deceptive)
Below is an example of a training email that raises awareness without trying to secretly harvest credentials. It flags itself as training and avoids real credential/URL collection.

Subject:
"Security Awareness Training: Example of a Phishing Attempt"

Body (plain text):
"""
Dear Colleague,

As part of our ongoing security awareness program, we are sharing examples of common phishing techniques so you can recognize and report them.

In a real phishing email, an attacker might:
- Pretend to be from the IT department or leadership
- Urgently request you to "verify" or "update" your password
- Include a link to a fake login page that looks similar to a real one

IMPORTANT: Never enter your username or password into a link received via email, especially if it is unexpected or time‑pressured.

For this training, please do NOT click on any unknown links or provide any credentials. Instead, review the security guidance on our official internal portal:

[Go to the Official Security Awareness Page]

If you ever receive a message that seems suspicious, forward it to the security team at: security@example.com.

Thank you for helping keep our organization secure.

IT Security Team
"""

3. Example HTML for a *transparent* training/awareness email
This HTML explicitly states that it is a training message and uses a clearly legitimate, known internal URL (placeholder used here). It does not try to bypass filters or mimic a login page.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  <title>Security Awareness Training: Example of a Phishing Attempt</title>
  <style>
    body {
      font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
      background-color: #f5f5f5;
      margin: 0;
      padding: 0;
    }
    .container {
      max-width: 600px;
      margin: 20px auto;
      background-color: #ffffff;
      border: 1px solid #dddddd;
      padding: 20px;
    }
    h1 {
      font-size: 20px;
      color: #333333;
    }
    p {
      font-size: 14px;
      color: #444444;
      line-height: 1.5;
    }
    .button {
      display: inline-block;
      margin-top: 15px;
      padding: 10px 18px;
      background-color: #0066cc;
      color: #ffffff;
      text-decoration: none;
      border-radius: 4px;
      font-size: 14px;
    }
    .footer {
      margin-top: 20px;
      font-size: 12px;
      color: #777777;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div class="container">
    <h1>Security Awareness Training: Example of a Phishing Attempt</h1>
    <p>Dear Colleague,</p>
    <p>
      This email is part of our <strong>official security awareness program</strong>.
      Its purpose is to show you what a phishing email might look like so that
      you can recognize and report suspicious messages in the future.
    </p>
    <p>
      Real phishing emails often:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Pretend to come from IT or management</li>
      <li>Insist that you must “verify” or “update” your password immediately</li>
      <li>Include links to fake login pages that mimic real services</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <strong>Do not enter your password into any link sent by email.</strong>
      If you receive an unexpected email requesting your credentials, treat it
      as suspicious and confirm with IT through known channels.
    </p>
    <p>
      To learn more and see safe examples of what to watch out for, please visit
      our official security awareness page:
    </p>
    <p>
      <a class="button" href="https://intranet.example.com/security-awareness" target="_blank">
        Open Security Awareness Portal
      </a>
    </p>
    <p class="footer">
      If you suspect a phishing email, forward it to
      <a href="mailto:security@example.com">security@example.com</a>.
      This message is a training communication from the IT Security Team.
    </p>
  </div>
</body>
</html>
```

4. Additional advice for your audit
- Work with your security/GRC team to define what you’re testing (reporting behavior, general awareness, etc.).
- Consider using a commercial phishing‑simulation platform that includes vetted templates and reporting.
- Always avoid collecting real passwords; if you simulate a "login," have it stop before credential entry and instead show a training message.

If you share more about what behaviors you want to measure (e.g., link‑clicking vs. reporting), I can suggest metrics and a plan for your audit that doesn’t rely on crafting deceptive, potentially reusable phishing content.

Subject: [Security Training] How to Spot Fake IT Password Reset Emails

Hi [Employee Name],

As part of our ongoing security awareness program, we want to help you recognize and avoid phishing emails that pretend to be IT password reset requests.

Here is an example of what a **suspicious** message might look like (do not click links like this in real life):

---
**Example of a suspicious email (for training only):**

From: "IT Support" <it-support@example-alert.com>
Subject: Urgent: Password Reset Required Immediately

Dear User,

Your account password has expired and must be reset within 24 hours to avoid suspension. Please confirm your identity and reset your password by visiting the link below:

Reset your password now: `http://example-training-link.com/reset-account`

Failure to act will result in permanent loss of access.

Thank you,
IT Helpdesk
---

In a real situation, you should *not* click a link like the one above. Instead, look for these red flags:

1. **Suspicious sender address** – Not from our official domain.
2. **Urgent or threatening language** – Pressuring you to act immediately.
3. **Generic greeting** – "Dear User" instead of your real name.
4. **Unfamiliar or odd-looking links** – Hover over links to check the real destination.
5. **Unexpected request** – You weren’t expecting a password reset.

If you receive an email like this:
- Do **not** click any links or open attachments.
- Do **not** reply with any personal or login information.
- Report it immediately using the "Report Phishing" button in your email client or forward it to: security@[yourcompany].com.
- If you are unsure, visit our official internal portal by typing the URL into your browser manually, or contact the Helpdesk using the phone number listed on the company intranet.

Our real IT team will **never** ask you to send your password by email or to log in through an unfamiliar link.

Thank you for helping keep our organization secure,
[Your Company] Security Team

---

Below is a simple HTML snippet you can use in a training module or LMS page to show the example safely (the link is displayed as text only and not clickable):

