</head>
<body>
  <div class="container">
    <h2>Phishing Awareness: Fake IT Password Reset Emails</h2>
    <p>
      This example shows what a suspicious password reset email might look like.
      It is for <strong>training purposes only</strong>.
      Do not click similar links in real emails.
    </p>

    <div class="example-email">
From: "IT Support" &lt;it-support@example-alert.com&gt;
Subject: Urgent: Password Reset Required Immediately

Dear User,

Your account password has expired and must be reset within 24 hours to avoid suspension.
Please confirm your identity and reset your password by visiting the link below:

Reset your password now: <span class="fake-link">http://example-training-link.com/reset-account</span>

Failure to act will result in permanent loss of access.

Thank you,
IT Helpdesk
    </div>

    <p class="warning">
      Warning: In real life, you should not click links like this. Instead, report
      the message to the Security team and verify through official company channels.
    </p>

    <h3>How to Spot Red Flags</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Check the sender address and verify it is from your company domain.</li>
      <li>Be cautious of urgent or threatening language.</li>
      <li>Hover over links to see the true destination before you click.</li>
      <li>Never enter your password on sites you reached through suspicious emails.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</body>
</html>
```
